        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-87 was the 24th flight of the Space Shuttle Columbia and the 88th flight of the Space Shuttle Program.  The orbiter crew included Commander Kevin R. Kregel, Pilot Steven W. Linsey, Mission Specialists Winston E. Scott, Kalpana Chawla, and Takao Doi, and Payload Specialist Leonid K. Kadenyuk. The launch proceeded as planned on the scheduled day and lifted off from Kennedy Space Center (KSC) on November 19, 1997.<P>The primary payload on STS-87 was the U.S. Microgravity Payload-4, a Spacelab project designed to gain additional information on the basic properties and behavior of various materials and liquids being flown in space.  Observations made from experiments of this payload will help produce better semiconductors for complex computers and other high-tech electronics.<P>Life sciences payloads on this mission included the Collaborative Ukrainian Experiment (CUE).  This payload was a collection of ten space biology plant experiments on topics ranging from the effects of microgravity on amino acid structuring to the susceptibility of plants to fungal pathogens in space.  The primary objective of these experiments was to discover what developmental events during plant reproduction fail to function normally in microgravity.  A secondary objective was to compare pollination and fertilization processes in microgravity with ground controls.  Primary experiments included Brassica rapa Photosynthetic Apparatus in Chamber (B-PAC) , <I>Brassica rapa</I> Seed Terminal growth In Chamber (B-STIC), Soybean Metabolism (SOYMET), Soybean-Pathogen Interactions (SOYPAT), and Space Moss Experiment (SPM). Secondary experiments included Amino Acid Content Experiment (AMINO), Gene Expression Experiment (GENEX), Lipid Content Experiment (LIPID), Phytohormonal Content Experiment (PHYTO), and Gravity effects on structure, function and organization of root cells in Brassica rapa (ROOTS).  The B-PAC experiment included an educational component involving the evaluation of the effects of microgravity on the pollination and fertilization of Brassica rapa seedlings.  Over 600,000 students in both the United States and the Ukraine performed experiments similar to the B-PAC performed in space by the Ukrainian Cosmonaut.<P>Another activity that took place during the mission was the deployment of the SPARTAN 201, a Solar Physics Spacecraft designed to perform remote sensing of the hot outer layers of the sun's atmosphere or corona..  Difficulties during flight forced retrieval of the vehicle by hand, and none of SPARTAN's scheduled research was performed.<P>The Space Shuttle Columbia touched down at KSC on December 5, 1997.<END>
        </div>

